cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use FaunaPulse in your research, please cite the software using the metadata provided below. Source code and documentation at https://github.com/valentinitnelav/fauna-pulse"
title: "FaunaPulse: a smartphone application for on-device detection, tracking and identification of animals"
abstract: >-
  FaunaPulse is a free, open-source Android application that turns a smartphone
  into an automated offline camera for monitoring animal activity.
  It detects and tracks animals in real time inside a user-defined region of
  interest (for example a flower, a feeding site or a nest entrance), using a
  replaceable object-detection model, and logs every detection with its time, so
  that visitation frequency and duration can be estimated. Capture also works
  without AI (motion-triggered photos, time-lapse photos including at night,
  scheduled videos), and detection can run afterwards on saved photos, or with
  tracking on saved or imported videos. An identification step then names the
  tracked animals of a whole session in bulk, combining several saved photos of
  each animal into one answer with a confidence per taxonomic rank. It uses
  BioCLIP 2 or 2.5 (vision foundation models that choose from a list of
  candidate taxa: ready-made name lists, for example for the main pollinator
  orders of Europe or the mammals and birds of the world, or a list prepared by
  the user) or a fixed-class insect classifier. Detection, tracking and
  identification all run on the phone: once the models are on it, no internet
  connection or cloud service is needed.
  Source code and documentation at https://github.com/valentinitnelav/fauna-pulse
  Compatible models for detection and identification are available at
  https://huggingface.co/collections/Valen-Peregrine/faunapulse
